## What is Git?

Git is a free, open-source version control system used to track changes in source code.

- It helps developers track code changes.
- It allows us to go back to previous versions of code.
- It helps multiple developers work on the same project.
- It works locally on our computer.

## What is GitHub?

GitHub is an online platform used to store and manage Git repositories.

- It allows developers to upload and share their code.
- It helps developers collaborate on projects.
- It provides cloud storage for code.
- It allows developers to contribute to open-source projects.

## Difference between Git and GitHub

| Git                         | GitHub                       |
| --------------------------- | ---------------------------- |
| Version control tool        | Online code hosting platform |
| Works locally               | Works online                 |
| Tracks code changes         | Stores Git repositories      |
| Developed by Linus Torvalds | Owned by Microsoft           |

## Common Git Commands

| Command                   | Purpose                     |
| ------------------------- | --------------------------- |
| `git init`                | Initialize a Git repository |
| `git add .`               | Stage all changes           |
| `git commit -m "message"` | Save changes                |
| `git status`              | Check repository status     |
| `git push`                | Upload code to GitHub       |
| `git pull`                | Download latest changes     |
| `git clone`               | Copy a repository           |
| `git log`                 | View commit history         |

Simple example:

Suppose you create a Java project.

1. Use Git to track changes in your Java code.
2. Commit your changes using Git.
3. Push your project to GitHub.
4. Now you can access your project online from anywhere.

Remember: Git is a tool to track code changes, whereas GitHub is a platform to store and collaborate on Git projects.